Slovenian law contains no offence of hitchhiking (''avtoštop''), and on ordinary roads it is legal. Article 30 of the ''Zakon o pravilih cestnega prometa'' (ZPrCP), [https://www.uradni-list.si/_pdf/2010/Ur/u2010109.pdf published in Uradni list RS 109/2010], is the motorway article: paragraph 1 admits only drivers of motor vehicles that can exceed 60 km/h onto an ''avtocesta'' or ''hitra cesta'', paragraph 2 allows joining and leaving only at the marked junctions, and paragraph 15 states flatly that "pedestrians must not step onto a motorway or expressway or walk along it". The exceptions in paragraph 16 are police, accident investigators, medics and firefighters, road inspectors and the like — not travellers. So the carriageway, the hard shoulder and the slip road are all closed, and what is left on the motorway is the ''počivališče'' rest areas and their petrol stations, where drivers have stopped anyway. On ''glavne'' and ''regionalne ceste'' hitchhiking is unregulated: stand on the verge where a driver can see you and pull over safely.
